It runs on this entire private system that's completely opaque,

688
00:41:49,920 --> 00:41:53,360
that runs alongside the internet that you have to pay to access.

689
00:41:53,360 --> 00:41:56,240
And also they can kick you off at any moment for any reason

690
00:41:56,240 --> 00:41:59,760
because net neutrality also first off doesn't apply to the U.S. anymore,

691
00:41:59,760 --> 00:42:02,880
but even if it did, does not apply to private networks.

692
00:42:02,880 --> 00:42:08,000
So you get into this place very quickly where we have an internet

693
00:42:08,000 --> 00:42:11,840
where pretty much anyone in a few positions of power

694
00:42:11,840 --> 00:42:13,920
can monkey with how the whole system works,

695
00:42:13,920 --> 00:42:16,560
let alone the U.S. government can just say,

696
00:42:16,560 --> 00:42:20,160
hey, and we saw the emails, Facebook, take down these posts.

697
00:42:20,160 --> 00:42:22,960
Hey, YouTube, remove this video.

698
00:42:22,960 --> 00:42:29,120
And that is a difference in sending a letter to Facebook saying delete this content

699
00:42:29,120 --> 00:42:31,840
and sending a letter to YouTube saying delete this content

700
00:42:31,840 --> 00:42:37,680
and sending a letter to Verizon saying lock this content

701
00:42:37,680 --> 00:42:41,760
is the only difference there is the political will of the United States

702
00:42:41,760 --> 00:42:44,560
versus the political will of the Chinese Communist Party.

703
00:42:44,560 --> 00:42:48,480
And I don't want to hang my hat on that differentiation

704
00:42:48,480 --> 00:42:52,240
in an era where free speech is kind of a little bit less than it used to be.

705
00:42:52,240 --> 00:42:55,680
You compare the 90s to the 2020s in terms of free speech

706
00:42:55,680 --> 00:42:58,800
and we have less of it now than we used to in a lot of ways.

707
00:42:58,800 --> 00:43:03,360
So this is a long way of saying when we think about these types of systems

708
00:43:03,360 --> 00:43:06,320
over the long term, I want credibly neutral alternatives

709
00:43:06,320 --> 00:43:08,560
to the existing communication systems

710
00:43:08,560 --> 00:43:12,080
and I want basically universal systems of prioritization.

711
00:43:12,080 --> 00:43:16,320
So today it is very hard to say if I'm Epic Games

712
00:43:16,320 --> 00:43:18,720
and I want to prioritize all the Fortnite traffic

713
00:43:18,800 --> 00:43:21,680
so kids can play more games and buy more skins.

714
00:43:21,680 --> 00:43:23,520
My bottom line revenue can go up.

715
00:43:23,520 --> 00:43:26,400
I basically have to go around and bribe every ISP to do that.

716
00:43:26,400 --> 00:43:28,160
You'd think there'd be a protocol on the internet

717
00:43:28,160 --> 00:43:31,360
that says I want to pay more to make my data better.

718
00:43:31,360 --> 00:43:32,560
You can't do that today.

719
00:43:32,560 --> 00:43:36,480
So double zero really over a long term time horizon

720
00:43:36,480 --> 00:43:39,040
our goal is to become the default prioritization layer

721
00:43:39,040 --> 00:43:42,800
for anyone anywhere that needs to send data in a timely fashion.

722
00:43:42,800 --> 00:43:45,200
I think the other piece we're looking at over the long term

723
00:43:45,200 --> 00:43:50,400
is replacing TCP IP with public key, private key encryption and verification.

724
00:43:50,400 --> 00:43:53,200
Right now, the only way that I know a server is correct

725
00:43:53,200 --> 00:43:55,760
is I'm verifying a SSL certificate

726
00:43:55,760 --> 00:43:58,080
which is issued by a third party authority.

727
00:43:58,080 --> 00:44:01,840
I actually don't care about routing to an IP address on a blockchain.

728
00:44:01,840 --> 00:44:04,560
I don't actually want to talk to the specific IP address.

729
00:44:04,560 --> 00:44:07,440
I want to talk to this specific cryptographic signature.

730
00:44:07,440 --> 00:44:11,680
So we can just cut out the entire middle layer for lack of a better term

731
00:44:11,760 --> 00:44:16,320
and actually start routing things through public, private key cryptography.

732
00:44:16,320 --> 00:44:18,720
And I think that is going to be a pretty big unlock

733
00:44:18,720 --> 00:44:22,240
for the ability to unwind a lot of these centralized,

734
00:44:22,240 --> 00:44:23,920
we literally call it a trust authority.

735
00:44:23,920 --> 00:44:25,280
That's what a CA is.

736
00:44:25,280 --> 00:44:28,880
We can try and unwind some of these centralized trust components

737
00:44:28,880 --> 00:44:31,520
from the system and actually increase competition.

738
00:44:31,520 --> 00:44:34,080
At the end of the day, I'm a capitalist maxi

739
00:44:34,080 --> 00:44:37,440
and I want more competition and fewer access barriers

740
00:44:37,440 --> 00:44:39,840
to building systems that compete with one another.

741
00:44:40,400 --> 00:44:43,440
To those points, do you think these base infrastructure layers

742
00:44:43,440 --> 00:44:46,720
are inherently monopolistic if not pushed otherwise

743
00:44:46,720 --> 00:44:50,560
to the point of first off, higher powers will want to control

744
00:44:50,560 --> 00:44:53,680
and they can do so via the base infrastructure.

745
00:44:53,680 --> 00:44:56,000
Secondarily, there aren't a lot of innovators.

746
00:44:56,000 --> 00:45:00,320
Do you think that's just what we converge on if not for other efforts?

747
00:45:01,040 --> 00:45:04,000
I think that is the story of every economic system

748
00:45:04,000 --> 00:45:06,320
going back as long as we've had an economy.

749
00:45:06,320 --> 00:45:07,920
It's like a central bank almost.

750
00:45:08,560 --> 00:45:09,520
Exactly, yes.

751
00:45:09,520 --> 00:45:12,560
There's two things we've done very intentionally to combat this.

752
00:45:12,560 --> 00:45:15,040
The first is it's a token project

753
00:45:15,040 --> 00:45:19,360
and the beauty of a token project is no one owns Bitcoin.

754
00:45:19,360 --> 00:45:20,800
Yeah, there's a bunch of people who maintain it

755
00:45:20,800 --> 00:45:22,800
and they have more power over it

756
00:45:22,800 --> 00:45:25,920
and Michael Saylor has got more power over Bitcoin than I do

757
00:45:25,920 --> 00:45:28,880
but at the end of the day, it is a giant consensus

758
00:45:28,880 --> 00:45:30,960
on how these things will work and function

759
00:45:30,960 --> 00:45:33,600
and that applies to Ethereum, that applies to Solana.

760
00:45:33,600 --> 00:45:36,640
You can't block someone on a Bitcoin to your point earlier.

761
00:45:36,720 --> 00:45:40,240
You can't block someone on Bitcoin or you can block someone on Bitcoin

762
00:45:40,240 --> 00:45:45,680
but it requires you getting 51% of the rest of the Bitcoin network on board with you.

763
00:45:45,680 --> 00:45:48,080
These systems are still built on trust

764
00:45:48,080 --> 00:45:50,720
but they're built on the trust of the crowd

765
00:45:50,720 --> 00:45:55,120
as opposed to the trust of an individual privileged actor in a system.

766
00:45:55,120 --> 00:45:58,160
You can still change something in the Ethereum ledger

767
00:45:58,160 --> 00:46:02,320
but the amount of people you need to get on board to facilitate that change,

768
00:46:02,320 --> 00:46:03,840
it's like amending the constitution.

769
00:46:03,840 --> 00:46:07,680
It's a very high barrier and that actually creates a ton of security.

770
00:46:07,680 --> 00:46:11,040
The second component for us is this proof of utility system

771
00:46:11,040 --> 00:46:14,720
where proof of stake systems can turn into an oligarchy at some point

772
00:46:14,720 --> 00:46:17,680
where you just have, oh, I have 4% of Solana

773
00:46:17,680 --> 00:46:22,320
so therefore I earn a ton of rewards even though I'm not doing a ton of work.

774
00:46:22,320 --> 00:46:25,760
With the proof of utility system, it's very similar to Bitcoin.

775
00:46:25,760 --> 00:46:28,240
If you have 10% of the hash power of Bitcoin,

776
00:46:28,240 --> 00:46:32,480
you're going to get 10% of the new blocks irrespective of if you have

777
00:46:32,480 --> 00:46:35,280
Michael Saylor bags or if you own zero Bitcoin.

778
00:46:35,280 --> 00:46:39,520
So we've tried to build our system in a way that encourages long-term competition

779
00:46:39,520 --> 00:46:44,320
and it also rewards folks who are able to put in that little incremental advantage.

780
00:46:44,320 --> 00:46:47,680
And actually I think the true mark of success for a system

781
00:46:47,680 --> 00:46:50,640
is if someone with a better idea or better technology

782
00:46:50,640 --> 00:46:52,960
can plug into that system permissionlessly

783
00:46:52,960 --> 00:46:56,240
and earn outsized rewards for outsized contribution.

784
00:46:56,240 --> 00:46:57,440
Yeah, I completely agree.

785
00:46:57,440 --> 00:46:59,040
Austin, it's been a pleasure chatting.

